Here’s a faster, safer approach and a compact VEX implementation. Key optimizations used:
- Avoid repeated expensive point() lookups by importing neighbor attributes through the point cloud handle (pcimport).
- Don’t update neighbor points inside the neighbor loop (setpointattrib on arbitrary points inside the loop is costly and causes contention). Instead compute a single offset for the current point and apply one write at the end.
- Do only one side of each pair: skip neighbors with ptnum <= @ptnum so each pair is processed once (halves work and avoids conflicting writes).
- Limit the maximum neighbors returned by pcopen (don’t use npoints(geoself()) as max unless you really need it).

Place this in a Point Wrangle (Run Over: Points):

// parameters
float separation_radius = chf("separation_radius");
int max_neighbors = chi("max_neighbors"); // e.g. 64

// open point cloud
int handle = pcopen(geoself(), "P", @P, separation_radius, max_neighbors);
vector total_offset = {0,0,0};
int found;
while ((found = pciterate(handle)) >= 0) {
    vector p2;
    pcimport(handle, "P", p2);                 // neighbor position (fast)
    int strand2;
    pcimport(handle, "strand_id", strand2);    // neighbor strand_id via pcimport (fast)

    // skip same strand
    if (strand2 == @strand_id) continue;

    // only handle each pair once
    if (found <= @ptnum) continue;

    float dist = distance(@P, p2);
    if (dist <= 1e-6) continue; // avoid divide-by-zero

    if (dist < separation_radius) {
        vector dir = (@P - p2) / dist; // normalized direction
        // accumulate offset (this point moves half the required separation)
        total_offset += dir * (separation_radius - dist) * 0.5;
    }
}
pcclose(handle);

// apply a single write for this point
if (length(total_offset) > 0) {
    vector newP = @P + total_offset;
    setpointattrib(geoself(), "P", @ptnum, newP, "set");
}

Notes and tuning:
- Set max_neighbors to a reasonable value (e.g. 32–128). A large max slows pcopen and wastes work; if you need to guarantee checking all possible neighbors within the radius, set it higher but test performance.
- If pcimport("strand_id", strand2) does not work for your attribute name, you can import the neighbor point number with pcimport(handle, "point.number", found) and then a single point() call outside the hot loop — but that’s slower. The shown pattern is the fastest when pcimport accepts the attribute.
- If you want both points to move (rather than just the one with higher ptnum), run this wrangle once and then run another pass swapping the comparison or run a solver/feedback setup. The one-sided approach avoids conflicting writes in a single point wrangle and is usually sufficient.

This pattern should significantly reduce the cost compared to doing point() lookups for every neighbor and writing neighbor positions inside the inner loop.

